  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so we have zero information about which design axes (modality, encoder, profile, aggregation, fusion) matter or what performance floor to expect. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the most minimal configuration in the space—it uses the simplest RNA encoder (PCA), a single profile (no aggregation needed), and no image or fusion. It establishes the absolute performance baseline and calibrates how much headroom more complex choices (learned encoders, multiprofile aggregation, multimodal fusion) can provide. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: With no prior data, the highest-value action is to anchor the search with the simplest interpretable reference. Any subsequent improvement (e.g., swapping PCA for scGPT, adding multiprofile aggregation, or introducing an image modality) can then be attributed to a specific design change rather than confounded with baseline difficulty. Starting with a complex multimodal config would make it impossible to decompose where gains or losses originate. A clean, minimal baseline maximizes the information gained per experiment under a limited budget."
